{{Listen|pos=right|filename=Maria Maria sample.ogg|type=music|title="Maria Maria"|description=A 25-second sample of the chorus of "Maria Maria", illustrating its [[Latin music|Latin]]-inspired [[Hip hop music|hip hop]] beat and [[Carlos Santana]]'s central guitar riff.<ref name="taylor"/>|format=[[ogg]]}}
Jean and Duplessis produced "Maria Maria" while the Product G&B provided the lead vocals.<ref name="albumnotes"/><ref name="cirisano">{{cite magazine|url=https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/product-gb-maria-maria-wild-thoughts-sample-interview-7849262/|title=Product G&B Talk Recording 'Maria Maria' & Being Sampled on 'Wild Thoughts' 17 Years Later: 'It's a Blessing'|last=Cirisano|first=Tatiana|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|date=June 29, 2017|access-date=July 17, 2022|archive-date=July 18, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220718012424/https://www.billboard.com/music/music-news/product-gb-maria-maria-wild-thoughts-sample-interview-7849262/|url-status=live}}</ref> Musically, "Maria Maria" is a [[Hip hop music|hip hop]] song with [[Latin music|Latin]] influences, punctuated by [[classical guitar|Spanish guitars]] and Carlos Santana's electric guitar.<ref name="taylor">{{cite magazine|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=dwgEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA31|title=Reviews & Previews: Singles|editor-last=Taylor|editor-first=Chuck|editor-link=Chuck Taylor (music journalist)|magazine=Billboard|volume=111|issue=43|page=31|date=October 23, 1999|access-date=July 18, 2022}}</ref> Tom Breihan of online publication ''[[Stereogum]]'' called the recording a "rap song [...] with no actual rapping".<ref name="breihan"/>

The song describes a woman named Maria who observes the tumultuous circumstances of the world around her and wishes for a more pleasurable existence. Santana himself sings additional vocals, performing the "''Ahora vengo mama chula''" chants, as does Jean, who created his intermittent lyrics by [[freestyle rap|freestyling]]. Duplessis plays the bass guitar while he and Jean collaborated on the drum programming.<ref name="breihan"/> Additional instrumentalists who contributed to the recording include Jeremy Cohen on violin, Daniel Seidenberg and [[David Balakrishnan|Hari Balakrishnan]] on viola, and Joseph Hébert on cello. The track was mixed, engineered, and mastered at three studios in New York City and at Fantasy Studios in [[Berkeley, California]].<ref name="albumnotes"/>

sections.text:
{{Listen|pos=right|filename=Maria Maria sample.ogg|type=music|title="Maria Maria"|description=A 25-second sample of the chorus of "Maria Maria", illustrating its Latin-inspired hip hop beat and Carlos Santana's central guitar riff.|format=ogg}}
Jean and Duplessis produced "Maria Maria" while the Product G&B provided the lead vocals. Musically, "Maria Maria" is a hip hop song with Latin influences, punctuated by Spanish guitars and Carlos Santana's electric guitar. Tom Breihan of online publication Stereogum called the recording a "rap song [...] with no actual rapping".

The song describes a woman named Maria who observes the tumultuous circumstances of the world around her and wishes for a more pleasurable existence. Santana himself sings additional vocals, performing the "Ahora vengo mama chula" chants, as does Jean, who created his intermittent lyrics by freestyling. Duplessis plays the bass guitar while he and Jean collaborated on the drum programming. Additional instrumentalists who contributed to the recording include Jeremy Cohen on violin, Daniel Seidenberg and Hari Balakrishnan on viola, and Joseph Hébert on cello. The track was mixed, engineered, and mastered at three studios in New York City and at Fantasy Studios in Berkeley, California.

